---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = "/compare/activecampaign-vs-klaviyo";
const title = "ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo: assign account and commerce ownership";
const description = "Compare a wholesale sales handoff with a Shopify event-driven launch, including SMS entitlements, cancellation and operating costs.";
const faqs = [{"question": "Does ActiveCampaign need an external integration for all SMS?", "answer": "No. ActiveCampaign documents its own SMS add-on. Confirm your plan, destinations, sender setup and required functions; do not infer availability from the base marketing subscription."}, {"question": "Which should own wholesale campaigns?", "answer": "Use the account approval and rep-handoff fixture to decide. Identify how account state reaches each contact, and prove that revocation cancels pending messages without granting channel permission."}, {"question": "Can we retain both platforms?", "answer": "Yes, if each job and state change has a named owner. Rehearse consent withdrawal, purchase exits, connector failure and replay before enabling overlapping campaigns."}, {"question": "Which is cheaper?", "answer": "Compare dated quotes for the same audience, channels, destinations and peak volume, including add-ons and integration labor. This page establishes no universal cost winner."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={description} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/compare">← All comparisons</a>
    <h1>ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo: who owns the customer decision?</h1>
    <p>A wholesale buyer may belong to an account with a rep, approval stage and negotiated terms. A retail shopper may qualify because of a product event and an individual permission record. Start this comparison with the decision your campaign must make: account and sales ownership, or commerce profile and event ownership. Neither model earns a universal campaign winner.</p>
    <h2>What the documented capabilities establish</h2>
    <p>ActiveCampaign is not limited to external SMS integrations. Its <a href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/14251311251356-Overview-of-ActiveCampaign-plans">plan overview</a> lists an SMS add-on for broadcasts and automation messages on Plus, Professional and Enterprise, and separate enhanced CRM add-ons. Its <a href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/20317088889372-Automation-triggers-explained">trigger documentation</a> describes deal-stage automation with those CRM entitlements. Check the quote for both the messaging and sales workflow you need.</p>
    <p>Nor is Shopify data exclusive to Klaviyo: ActiveCampaign lists Shopify among its <a href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/115001165770-Which-ecommerce-solutions-have-Deep-Data-Integrations">Deep Data integrations</a>. The useful distinction is how your implementation combines sales state with store events, not an unsupported claim that one platform has no commerce data.</p>
    <p>Klaviyo’s <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360030919351">Shopify sync instructions</a> describe profile and subscription synchronization. They also distinguish message events from what is individually visible in Shopify. A shared customer record is a useful foundation for retention work, but it does not demonstrate that every consent change, purchase exit or pending action has synchronized correctly. The <a href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo evaluation</a> develops that profile/event contract.</p>
    <h2>Choose the ownership model before the feature checklist</h2>
    <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Campaign decision</th><th>Account/sales implementation</th><th>Commerce/profile implementation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Who qualifies?</td><td>Approved account, assigned rep and deal stage; identify which contacts inherit account eligibility</td><td>Product interest, order state and individual channel permission; identify the event that qualifies the profile</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Who can change the decision?</td><td>Sales owner approves or revokes access; marketing receives an auditable update</td><td>Store event changes eligibility; marketing owns the segment and pending message</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What cancels it?</td><td>Account suspended, deal closed or rep requests a pause</td><td>Purchase, stock loss, offer expiry or channel withdrawal</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What must survive a handoff?</td><td>Account identifier, contact role, approval and rep ownership</td><td>Profile identity, event identifier, product/variant and consent evidence</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <h2>Proposed fixture A: wholesale approval and sales follow-up</h2>
    <p>Create a test account with two buyer contacts, only one of whom has SMS permission. Put its deal into an approval stage, assign a rep and schedule an approved-account invitation. Before dispatch, revoke the account approval and change the rep. Record account and contact identifiers, triggering stage, automation enrollment, permission decision, pending-message identifier and cancellation result.</p>
    <p>The acceptance condition is precise: account approval must not subscribe the second buyer to SMS; revocation must stop the obsolete invitation; the new rep must see the current task rather than inherit a duplicate follow-up. Ask the ActiveCampaign operator to show the actual entitled deal trigger and contact enrollment behavior. Ask the Klaviyo operator to show how account changes enter the profile/event model and who maintains that connector. Neither implementation passes on a screenshot of a segment alone.</p>
    <h2>Proposed fixture B: commerce launch and purchase exit</h2>
    <p>Use controlled profiles for a first-time buyer, a repeat buyer, an email-only subscriber and a person whose phone number changes. Qualify them for a variant-specific early-access message. Then place an order, remove stock, replay the qualifying event and withdraw SMS permission while the message waits. Record event time separately from ingestion time, profile resolution, audience evaluation and dispatch.</p>
    <p>Require no promotional text to the email-only profile, no duplicate enrollment from the replay, and no stale stock or cart message after its cancellation condition. Ask each operator to show the event properties used and the pending action that was removed. These are proposed acceptance exercises; no merchant trial, conversion lift or vendor latency result is claimed.</p>
    <h2>Email and SMS coordination needs an explicit owner</h2>
    <p>If both systems remain, assign one sender to each message job and one authority to each state change. Write the direction of consent updates, purchase exits and account holds. Specify how an unavailable connector pauses sending and how a replay avoids duplicate messages. An email opt-out is not automatically the same decision as an SMS opt-out: preserve the recorded scope and apply the appropriate channel policy.</p>
    <p>Rehearse a permission withdrawal immediately before dispatch, a purchase arriving late and an unknown recipient timezone. Require evidence of cancellation or a documented stop procedure, plus an expiry for offers that cannot wait. Use the <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">coordination guide</a> for the broader operating calendar. A shared profile does not itself prove a combined frequency limit across tools.</p>
    <h2>Compare the cost of your accepted implementation</h2>
    <p>Request two dated quotes for the same eligible audience, email volume, destination mix, SMS usage and peak launch month. For ActiveCampaign, itemize the required CRM and SMS add-ons. For Klaviyo, ask how billable profiles, email allowance and text usage apply to your account. Include connector maintenance, seats, support, migration and the work needed to reconcile failed events. No fixed price or cheaper-at-scale claim follows from this comparison.</p>
    <p>Calculate total operating cost over the same period. Separately calculate incremental contribution from a controlled holdout: incremental net sales less product costs, incentives not already reflected in sales, message fees and incremental support costs. Do not subtract the same discount twice. Allocate fixed platform costs consistently before comparing alternatives; platform attribution alone cannot identify incremental sales.</p>
    <h2>A decision record your team can use</h2>
    <p>Record each fixture’s pass/fail result, evidence link, missing entitlement, repair owner and recurring maintenance estimate. Prefer the account/sales implementation when account approval and rep handoff drive the real campaign and its trace passes. Prefer the commerce/profile implementation when product events and individual lifecycle state drive the campaign and its trace passes. If both are required, price and rehearse the boundary rather than assuming a two-platform stack will coordinate itself.</p>
    <p>The sibling <a href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend comparison</a> follows a capture-to-send journey. This page instead tests account authority against commerce event authority. For a transport failure after an eligible send, use the <a href="/use-cases/deliverability">deliverability incident workflow</a>.</p>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions before implementation" />
</BaseLayout>
